==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|hidous}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|hidous}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|hideus}}, {{m|fro|hydus||that which inspires terror}}, from earlier {{m|fro|hisdos}}, from {{m|fro|hisda||horror, fear}}, of uncertain and disputed origin. Probably from {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*egisda}}, {{m|frk|*egisida||terror, fright}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*agisiþō||horror, terror}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*agisōną||to frighten, terrorise}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*agaz||terror, fear}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₂egʰ-||to frighten}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|egisa}}, {{m|goh|egidī||horror}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|egesa||fear, dread}}, {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃||fear, terror}}.

Alternative etymology cites possbile derivation from {{etyl|la|-}} {{m|la|hispidosus||rugged}}, from {{m|la|hispidus||rough, bristly}}, yet the semantic evolution is less plausible. <!--fro?: ''[[hidous]], [[hidos]], [[hidus]], [[hisdos]], [[hisdous]]'', {{etyl|fr|en}} ''[[hideux]]'': confer {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|hide|lang=fro}}, {{term|hisde||fright}}; , {{etyl|en|-}}-->

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈhɪd.iː.ʌs/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-hideous.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Frightful; shocking; extremely [[ugly]].
#: A piteous and hideous spectacle." [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# Distressing or offensive to the ear; horrible.
#* '''1719''', [[w:Daniel Defoe|Daniel Defoe]], ''[[w:Robinson Crusoe|Robinson Crusoe]]''
#*: ''He started up, growling at first, but finding his leg broken, fell down again; and then got upon three legs, and gave the most '''hideous''' roar that ever I heard.''
# Hateful; shocking.
#: ''Sure, you have some hideous matter to deliver.'' - [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]]

====Usage notes====
* Nouns to which "hideous" is often applied: monster, creature, man, face, thing, crime, form, death, aspect, spectacle, picture, roar, sound, manner, way, disease, mistake, shape, dress, fact, act, smile.

====Synonyms====
* [[frightful]], [[ghastly]], [[grim]], [[grisly]], [[grotesque]], [[horrid]], [[dreadful]], [[terrible]]

====Derived terms====
* [[hideosity]]
* [[hideously]]
* [[hideousness]]

